      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "STUDY DESIGN",
          "content": "Case report."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To report a long segment epidural hematoma with thoracic cord compression caused by a metastatic carcinoma."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "SUMMARY OF BACKGROUND DATA",
          "content": "To our knowledge, there have been no previous reports of spontaneous spinal epidural hematoma caused by metastatic carcinoma."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "A 60-year-old woman with back pain and progressive leg weakness suddenly developed complete paraplegia and anesthesia below T8. A thoracic spine MRI showed an epidural hematoma and metastatic tumor. We describe the clinical course, radiographic imaging, operative findings, and treatment results."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Despite immediate surgery, the patient failed to have any significant improvement in her neurologic symptoms. A previously unsuspected primary lung tumor was diagnosed. She died 6 months after spine surgery."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Spinal epidural hematoma can occur in association with metastatic tumors in that area."
        }
      ]},
